{
  "$comment": "AUTHORED SOURCE. This file is not generated. scripts/build-tokens.mts reads this file and emits app/tokens.generated.css and lib/generated/tokens.ts. Edit here, never there.",
  "version": 1,
  "namespace": "color",
  "cssPrefix": "--opsin",
  "cssNaming": {
    "step": "--opsin-{axis}-{name}-{step}",
    "role": "--opsin-{axis}-{name}-{role}",
    "neutralStep": "--opsin-neutral-{step}",
    "axis": {
      "categories": "category",
      "status": "status",
      "absence": "status"
    },
    "$comment": "Example: --opsin-category-heart-600 (primitive tier) and --opsin-status-urgent-ink (semantic tier). `absence.unknown` is emitted under the status axis so a component can switch on one variable name, but it is NOT a status level. See rules."
  },
  "rules": [
    "The two axes never mix. A surface may carry a category colour or a status colour, never both. Category answers 'what kind of reading is this'; status answers 'what should the reader do about it'. A card tinted by category and bordered by status makes the two indistinguishable at a glance and is the failure this system exists to prevent.",
    "Colour never carries status alone. Every status is rendered as colour PLUS an icon PLUS a word. The rendering must survive grayscale and every simulated colour-vision deficiency with its meaning intact.",
    "Category colour carries identity, never verdict. A heart-red card does not mean something is wrong with a heart reading.",
    "`accent` is a fill, never text and never the sole boundary. Text on a tinted surface uses `ink`; the boundary uses `line`. Both are chosen to clear the contrast floor; `accent` is chosen for identity and is not guaranteed to.",
    "Never reference a step directly from a component. Components consume roles. Steps are the primitive tier and may be re-tuned in a minor release; roles are covered by the versioning policy."
  ],
  "ladder": {
    "steps": [50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950],
    "lightness": {
      "50": 0.972,
      "100": 0.941,
      "200": 0.884,
      "300": 0.806,
      "400": 0.714,
      "500": 0.622,
      "600": 0.541,
      "700": 0.452,
      "800": 0.362,
      "900": 0.276,
      "950": 0.208
    },
    "chromaEnvelope": {
      "50": 0.14,
      "100": 0.24,
      "200": 0.42,
      "300": 0.66,
      "400": 0.88,
      "500": 1,
      "600": 0.97,
      "700": 0.88,
      "800": 0.74,
      "900": 0.58,
      "950": 0.44
    },
    "$comment": "One lightness ladder for every chromatic ramp, so that step 600 means the same lightness in every ramp and a component can swap category without changing its contrast behaviour. Chroma is the seed chroma multiplied by the envelope, then clamped into sRGB. The envelope peaks at 500 and falls away at both ends because high chroma at very high or very low lightness is out of gamut and reads as neon."
  },
  "gamut": {
    "base": "sRGB",
    "wide": "display-p3",
    "p3ChromaGain": 1.18,
    "$comment": "Every step is authored twice. `srgb` is clamped into the sRGB gamut and is what every browser gets. `p3`, when present, is the same lightness and hue at up to 18% more chroma, clamped into Display-P3, emitted by build-tokens.mts inside @media (color-gamut: p3). A step with no `p3` key is already at or above the P3 boundary for its lightness, so there is no wide-gamut variant to serve. `clampedToSrgb: true` records that the envelope asked for more chroma than sRGB can hold at that lightness. It is the honest version of 'this colour is as saturated as the display allows'."
  },
  "floor": {
    "text": {
      "apcaLc": 75,
      "wcag": 4.5,
      "appliesTo": "Body text and any text below 24px or below 18.66px bold."
    },
    "largeText": {
      "apcaLc": 60,
      "wcag": 3,
      "appliesTo": "Text at 24px and above, or 18.66px bold and above."
    },
    "nonText": {
      "apcaLc": 45,
      "wcag": 3,
      "appliesTo": "Boundaries, icons, chart strokes, focus rings and any graphic that carries meaning."
    },
    "$comment": "opsinjs publishes BOTH an APCA Lc figure and a WCAG 2.2 ratio for every pair, and a pair must clear both. APCA models real text rendering better; WCAG 2.2 is what a procurement reviewer and a VPAT ask for. The measured numbers live in lib/generated/contrast.json and are produced by scripts/check-contrast.mts. They are never written by hand and never quoted in prose. One copy in this file is the named exception: `colourIndependence.statusCollisions` is a rounded snapshot kept so that an author reading the palette does not have to open the generated file, it is not authoritative, and nothing diffs it against the generator. Where the two disagree, the generated file is right."
  },
  "roles": {
    "order": ["surface", "line", "ink", "accent"],
    "definitions": {
      "surface": "The tinted background a component in this ramp sits on.",
      "line": "The boundary, icon stroke or chart mark. The lightest value in the ramp that clears the non-text floor against `surface`.",
      "ink": "Text and text-sized icons on `surface`. Clears the text floor against `surface`.",
      "accent": "The identity fill: a bar fill, a dial track, a legend dot. Chosen for recognition, not for contrast. It must be bounded by `line` or labelled in `ink`; it is never the only thing that carries the meaning."
    },
    "mapping": {
      "light": {
        "surface": 50,
        "line": 600,
        "ink": 800,
        "accent": "seed"
      },
      "dark": {
        "surface": 950,
        "line": 300,
        "ink": 200,
        "accent": 400
      }
    },
    "$comment": "Steps chosen by measurement, not by eye: across all ten chromatic ramps, ink-on-surface measures Lc 88-90 in light. In dark, the surface is step 950 rather than step 900, a quieter slab that spends far less of the urgency budget than a saturated step-900 fill, and ink is step 200 rather than step 100, so a category label keeps enough chroma to be told apart by hue instead of collapsing to a near-white with every other label; the measured dark ink-on-surface figure is carried in the generated lib/generated/contrast.json. Line-on-surface measures Lc 67-71 in light; the dark figure tracks the surface step and is carried in the generated lib/generated/contrast.json rather than quoted here. `accent` in light is the design-direction seed used verbatim; in dark it is step 400, because the seed's lightness is chosen against a white page and reads as a hole on a dark one."
  },
